**Proposition 7.3 (exact RC parameter match and auxiliary normalization).**
On a sufficiently small positive RC endpoint segment the analytic
parameter of Sections 3–6 is EXACTLY

$$
\begin{aligned}
p(a)&=\frac2{\lambda_R(a)}
     =-\frac{2a\ell_{1R}}{g_RD_R^2\sigma_R},\\
\xi_R(a)&=\frac{2\beta a}{D_R},\qquad
p&=\frac{2\beta}{R_1}\xi_R+O(\xi_R^2)
  =\frac{4\beta^2}{\alpha R_1}a+O(a^2).
\end{aligned}
\tag{EP.48a}
$$

The four local contacts, masses and grouped matrices coincide with
RC's $q_+,q_-,n_R/D_R,Z_R/(Z_R)_0$ and their coordinate-$0$-one
weights, in corner order $(++),(+-),(-+),(--)$. In particular the
multiplier in EP.9 is RC.23's normalized $\lambda$, not the CG
multiplier, JR's spectral multiplier or a coefficient for a different
objective. The corresponding positive multiplier for $\mathcal T$
against $\tau$ is $\lambda/2$, and against physical $s$ it is
$\lambda/(2s_0)$. Thus their respective endpoint corrections are
$K_1$ and $K_1/s_0$.

Proof. RC64 gives $k(0)=-B/(4R_1)$ and
$\lambda_R\xi_R\to R_1/\beta>0$. Its formulas make
$\lambda_R\xi_R$ analytic and nonzero at zero: the explicit $a$
in the denominator cancels against $\xi_R$. Hence EP.48a extends
analytically across zero, with the displayed strictly positive first
derivative. It has an analytic local inverse. Both $R_1,R_2>0$ follow
from the coarse rational comparisons in Proposition 7.2.

To identify the FULL contact germ, use RC61's gauge
$Y_R=G+\lambda_RC+S_A$ with
$S_A=-2\lambda_RW_RW_R^T/(D_R^2\sigma_R)$. Then

$$
\frac p2(Y_R-G)=C-\frac{2W_RW_R^T}{D_R^2\sigma_R}=:A_R^{\rm raw}.
\tag{EP.48b}
$$

This matrix is analytic at $a=0$, where its polynomial on $(1,u,v)$
is $(1+u^2)/2$. If $b_{22}$ is its $22$ entry, add
$(b_{22}/\beta)Q$. This removes the $v^2$ coefficient without
changing any value on the null ellipse, its tangential derivatives,
its trace, or any null moment pairing. The resulting polynomial has
precisely the five monomials of Definition 3.1. On every RC contact,
the corresponding PSD slack has zero quadratic form and zero
tangential derivative. After multiplication by $p/2$, its scalar
contact equation is $P=|u|+p[-f]_+$ with the same strict signs as
EP.19. RC's normalized Gram gives all five moments EP.20; its
coordinate-$0$-one weights tend to $1/4$. Consequently these
polynomials, contacts and masses solve EP.20–21 analytically in $p$.
Lemma 3.2's uniqueness identifies them with the local germ. This proves
the exact match without inferring equality of instruments from agreement
only on test states.

The endpoint normalization in RC64 is essential. Its auxiliary vector
$c_{\rm aux}=Z_R/(\beta D_R^5)$ tends to $-c_-/4$, where
$c_-=(1,-1,-1)^T$, whereas the local contact is
$\widehat c=Z_R/(Z_R)_0\to c_-$. RC.51 gives, for its
$\mathcal H_R=C-W_RW_R^T/(D_R^2\sigma_R)$,

$$
\left.\frac{d}{d\xi_R}(\mathcal H_Rc_{\rm aux})\right|_0
=\tfrac14(1-3k(0),\ 1-3\alpha k(0),\ \beta k(0))^T.
\tag{EP.48c}
$$

The derivative for $\mathcal H_R\widehat c$ is $-4$ times this
vector. Indeed $\widehat c=t c_{\rm aux}$ with $t(0)=-4$, and
the product-rule term $t'\mathcal H_Rc_{\rm aux}$ vanishes at zero.
No coefficient is transferred between those two vector normalizations
without this factor. The scalar $k(0)$, $\lambda_R\xi_R$ and
$\Gamma$ are unchanged. EP.48a and the contact identification show that
EP.35 and Theorem 7.1 compute the next terms of the very same full
RC curve. The objective conversion EP.14 proves the final multiplier
scales stated above. $\square$

